### Why is falsification the mark of principal-level thinking?

Junior engineers defend decisions. Senior engineers falsify them. Each choice is a bet on assumptions, and the professional move is to make those assumptions explicit with measurable triggers.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class FalsificationMindset {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Junior: 'We should use Redis because everyone does.'");
        System.out.println("Senior: 'We should use Redis because our read hit rate is 95%");
        System.out.println("  and DB read utilization is at 80%. If hit rate drops below 60%");
        System.out.println("  or DB utilization drops below 30%, Redis becomes unnecessary.'");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("The second answer can be TESTED and REVISITED.");
        System.out.println("The first answer is fashion.");
    }
}
```

```text
Junior: 'We should use Redis because everyone does.'
Senior: 'We should use Redis because our read hit rate is 95%
  and DB read utilization is at 80%. If hit rate drops below 60%
  or DB utilization drops below 30%, Redis becomes unnecessary.'

The second answer can be TESTED and REVISITED.
The first answer is fashion.
```

### When would Redis be unnecessary?

Redis is justified only when there is a hot set of data read far more than written, the read volume exceeds what the database can serve, and the latency improvement justifies the operational cost.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class RedisJustification {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double hitRate = 0.95;
        double dbReadUtilization = 0.80;

        System.out.println("Cache hit rate: " + (hitRate * 100) + "%");
        System.out.println("DB read utilization: " + (dbReadUtilization * 100) + "%");
        System.out.println("Redis justified: YES (high hit rate, DB under pressure)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Evidence Redis is UNNECESSARY:");
        System.out.println("  - Hit rate < 60% (most reads miss, cache is just an extra hop)");
        System.out.println("  - DB utilization < 30% (DB's own buffer cache copes)");
        System.out.println("  - Data set < 100MB (in-process Caffeine suffices, no network hop)");
    }
}
```

```text
Cache hit rate: 95.0%
DB read utilization: 80.0%
Redis justified: YES (high hit rate, DB under pressure)

Evidence Redis is UNNECESSARY:
  - Hit rate < 60% (most reads miss, cache is just an extra hop)
  - DB utilization < 30% (DB's own buffer cache copes)
  - Data set < 100MB (in-process Caffeine suffices, no network hop)
```

### When would synchronous communication be preferable to async?

Async messaging assumes the caller does not need an immediate answer and can tolerate eventual consistency. When those assumptions fail, synchronous is better.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class AsyncVsSync {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Async justified when:");
        System.out.println("  - Caller does not need immediate result");
        System.out.println("  - Eventual consistency is acceptable");
        System.out.println("  - Need to decouple producer/consumer");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Evidence SYNC is preferable:");
        System.out.println("  - POS authorization: must approve/decline before customer leaves");
        System.out.println("  - Balance check: caller needs the answer NOW to proceed");
        System.out.println("  - Simple request-response: broker overhead buys nothing");
    }
}
```

```text
Async justified when:
  - Caller does not need immediate result
  - Eventual consistency is acceptable
  - Need to decouple producer/consumer

Evidence SYNC is preferable:
  - POS authorization: must approve/decline before customer leaves
  - Balance check: caller needs the answer NOW to proceed
  - Simple request-response: broker overhead buys nothing
```

### When would microservice boundaries be wrong?

Microservice boundaries assume independent scaling, deployment, and ownership along genuine domain seams.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class MicroserviceBoundaries {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Microservices justified when:");
        System.out.println("  - Services change for different reasons at different rates");
        System.out.println("  - Distinct scaling profiles");
        System.out.println("  - Separate teams own them with clear contracts");
        System.out.println("  - Cross-boundary coupling < 20%");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Evidence boundaries are WRONG:");
        System.out.println("  - Most features require coordinated deploys across 3+ services");
        System.out.println("  - One small team owns all services, drowning in distributed tax");
        System.out.println("  - Services share a database, coupling through it anyway");
        System.out.println("  → Modular monolith gives same separation without the overhead");
    }
}
```

```text
Microservices justified when:
  - Services change for different reasons at different rates
  - Distinct scaling profiles
  - Separate teams own them with clear contracts
  - Cross-boundary coupling < 20%

Evidence boundaries are WRONG:
  - Most features require coordinated deploys across 3+ services
  - One small team owns all services, drowning in distributed tax
  - Services share a database, coupling through it anyway
  → Modular monolith gives same separation without the overhead
```

### Why does stating falsification conditions build trust?

Three reasons: it proves the decision was reasoned, gives measurable triggers for revisiting, and builds trust because the designer volunteers weaknesses.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class TrustBuilding {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Reason 1: Proves decision was evidence-based, not fashion");
        System.out.println("  Only a reasoned decision has identifiable assumptions.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Reason 2: Gives concrete, measurable triggers to revisit");
        System.out.println("  'If hit rate < 60%, drop Redis' → objective, not political.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Reason 3: Builds trust with reviewers and leadership");
        System.out.println("  An engineer who volunteers weaknesses is reasoning honestly.");
    }
}
```

```text
Reason 1: Proves decision was evidence-based, not fashion
  Only a reasoned decision has identifiable assumptions.

Reason 2: Gives concrete, measurable triggers to revisit
  'If hit rate < 60%, drop Redis' → objective, not political.

Reason 3: Builds trust with reviewers and leadership
  An engineer who volunteers weaknesses is reasoning honestly.
```

### How do you formalize this with Architecture Decision Records?

Pair each assumption with the condition that would invalidate it, and review against live metrics on a cadence.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class ArchitectureDecisionRecord {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ADR-007: Use Redis for account balance cache");
        System.out.println("  Assumption: hit rate > 80%, DB read util > 60%");
        System.out.println("  Falsification: hit rate < 60% OR DB util < 30%");
        System.out.println("  Review cadence: quarterly");
        System.out.println("  Current metrics: hit rate 95%, DB util 80%");
        System.out.println("  Status: VALID");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("ADR-012: Microservice boundary for statement service");
        System.out.println("  Assumption: separate team, independent deploy, low coupling");
        System.out.println("  Falsification: >50% features require cross-service deploy");
        System.out.println("  Current metrics: 15% cross-service deploys");
        System.out.println("  Status: VALID");
    }
}
```

```text
ADR-007: Use Redis for account balance cache
  Assumption: hit rate > 80%, DB read util > 60%
  Falsification: hit rate < 60% OR DB util < 30%
  Review cadence: quarterly
  Current metrics: hit rate 95%, DB util 80%
  Status: VALID

ADR-012: Microservice boundary for statement service
  Assumption: separate team, independent deploy, low coupling
  Falsification: >50% features require cross-service deploy
  Current metrics: 15% cross-service deploys
  Status: VALID
```

### What are common mistakes?

Defending a decision by authority or fashion rather than evidence and falsifiable assumptions.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

// WRONG: "We use Redis because Netflix uses Redis."
// No assumptions stated, no triggers to revisit, no way to objectively evaluate

// CORRECT: "We use Redis because hit rate is 95% and DB read util is 80%.
// If hit rate drops below 60%, we will remove it."
// Evidence-based, falsifiable, reviewable
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you balance the cost of change against the cost of keeping a decision that may be wrong?
- What is an Architecture Decision Record and how do you use it?
- How do you decide when an assumption has been violated enough to warrant a redesign?
- What is the difference between a reversible and an irreversible architectural decision?
